Our main interest will lie with those partially ordered sets in which every subset has a lub: namely, *complete lattices*.  We use Mathlib’s `CompleteLattice` typeclass to express that “lattices are complete”.

If $D$ is such a space we write $\bot = \bigsqcup \emptyset$ and $\top = \bigsqcup D$ for the smallest and largest elements (read: bottom and top). As is well known, greatest lower bounds must exist, for:

$$
\sqcap S = \bigsqcup x \in D : x \sqsubseteq y \text{ for all } y \in S
$$

gives the definition.

Given a complete lattice $D$ we define

$$
x \ll y \text{ iff } y \in \text{Int}z \in D : x \sqsubseteq z,
$$

where the interior is taken in the sense of the induced topology.

The relation $x \ll y$ behaves somewhat like a strict ordering relation; at least its meaning is clearly that $y$ should be definitely larger than $x$ in the partial ordering. 

Following subsequent historical usage, we call this the "way below" relation.

Such a relation has many pleasant properties.

Proposition 2.2(vi). WayBelow.self_iff_scottOpen_Ici. `x ≪ x` iff the principal up-set `{z | x ⊑ z}` (i.e.
`Set.Ici x`) is Scott-open. This characterizes the *compact* (finite, isolated) elements:
`x` is compact exactly when `↑x` is open. 

Proposition 2.2(vii). WayBelow.sSup_iff. For a non-empty directed set `S`, `x ≪ ⊔S` iff
`x ≪ y` for some `y ∈ S`. The forward direction is exactly inaccessibility of a Scott-open
set; the backward direction is monotonicity on the right. 

`scottOpen_wayBelow` records that \(\{z \mid x \ll z\}\) is Scott-open. 

`directedOn_wayBelow` records that \(\{x \mid x \ll y\}\) is directed.

`sInf_wayBelow` records that the infimum of a Scott-open neighbourhood of \(y\) is way below \(y\). 
